≈ 1.10× |\n| 2 | 1195 ≈ 1.00× | 651 ≈ 1.055× |\n| 0 | 1137 ≈ 0.96× | 617 ≈ 1.00× |\n\n- **Candidate 1:** product 0 piles up, and holding it costs 0.7 per unit per day. Product 1 spoils, and the warehouse overflows. Clearly the worst.\n- **Candidate 2 vs. 6:** for product 0 the holding cost is high, and the extra stock in Candidate 6 eats up what it gains from fewer shortages. My estimate is about −200 for Candidate 6 against Candidate 2. For product 1 the two are roughly even.\n- **Candidate 0:** cheaper to buy and hold, but it pays more in shortage penalties. On product 0 it roughly ties with Candidate 2, and on product 1 it is a little worse.\n- **What sets Candidate 2 apart:** its first order is larger (105 / 55), which builds a buffer while stock is at its lowest. Its last orders are smaller (70 / 48, then 30 / 12), so it sells down the accumulated buffer instead of throwing it away at the end, since leftovers are worth nothing.\n- **Candidate 5 vs. 6:** Candidate 5 saves 58 by skipping the day-26 order. It pays about the same back in two extra days of holding, and its day-27 arrival of about 180 units plus leftovers risks overflowing the warehouse. It is essentially even with or slightly worse than Candidate 6.\n\nCandidate 2 sits at about the right order level for both products and ends the month most sensibly. Since its lead is small and demand is very random, the probability that it actually comes out best is about 0.4."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3832, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2304, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3832}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T03:11:02.950Z", "request_id": "req_011CgArsY